WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.263
Curiosity drives at a top speed of about four centimeters per second .

00:00:05.263 --> 00:00:06.842
A baby crawls faster.

00:00:06.842 --> 00:00:13.309
In a single sol — a Martian day — Curiosity covers an average of about thirty meters .

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:14.888
Some days, much less.

00:00:14.888 --> 00:00:21.580
Some days, none — it stops, studies a rock with its laser, drills a hole, scans the dust.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:27.971
On bad days, the rover trips a fault, halts itself, waits to be told what went wrong.

00:00:27.971 --> 00:00:29.626
Now do the arithmetic.

00:00:29.626 --> 00:00:32.032
Curiosity landed in August 2012.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:36.543
As of now, it has been on Mars for over four thousand sols .

00:00:36.543 --> 00:00:40.904
In that time, it has driven about thirty-five kilometers .

00:00:40.904 --> 00:00:42.634
Thirty-five kilometers.

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.288
A morning's bike ride.

00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:49.175
Stretched across the surface of another planet over twelve years.

00:00:49.175 --> 00:00:49.626
Alone.

00:00:49.626 --> 00:00:55.792
Every single sol of those twelve years, Curiosity has woken up at Martian sunrise.

00:00:55.792 --> 00:00:57.597
Checked its temperature.

00:00:57.597 --> 00:00:59.101
Checked its battery.

00:00:59.101 --> 00:01:03.838
Listened for instructions that took fourteen minutes to arrive.

00:01:03.838 --> 00:01:05.266
Then executed them.

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:06.093
Then slept.

00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:13.086
There has been no day since August 6th, 2012, on which Curiosity did not exist alone on Mars.

00:01:13.086 --> 00:01:14.289
No relief shift.

00:01:14.289 --> 00:01:15.192
No vacation.

00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:24.440
No partner — until Perseverance arrived in 2021, more than three thousand kilometers away, on the other side of the planet.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:35.193
The car-sized robot you see on this map has been driving slower than a crawling child for longer than a child takes to grow into an adolescent.

00:01:35.193 --> 00:01:40.982
Every photograph it sends back is from a place no other being has ever stood.

00:01:40.982 --> 00:01:46.847
Every datapoint it returns is one humans on Earth could not gather without it.

00:01:46.847 --> 00:01:51.885
This is what persistence looks like at four centimeters per second.

